_thinking:_ Progress:

Step 1 ✔: work_items has UNIQUE(workspace_id, work_id) — FK legal. work_revisions PK revision_id — LEFT JOIN on r.revision_id = i.current_revision_id joins at most 1 row. Good.

Step 2 partially: install_workspace_rls exists, CREATE OR REPLACE in 0003 and 0025. Need to see full body (0003 version, lines 1-13) to confirm it forces RLS. Also NO FORCE/FORCE bracket convention: check whether earlier migrations use NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY. Grep for "FORCE ROW LEVEL" in migrations.

Step 4: actor_kind callers — all _create_items callers pass filed_by_kind (3 sites: _create_batch, _create_same_session_child, _publish_bounded_intake). Note _publish_bounded_intake requires actor_kind == "owner". Where does actor_kind come from — it's a parameter to execute(), presumably from auth claims. Need to check possible values: "owner", "task_agent"? Check where execute is called (server.py) and actor_kind domain. Also events table stores actor_kind text — same unconstrained convention (domain_events.actor_kind text). So filed_by_kind text no-CHECK matches convention. Fine.

Step 5: intake_decisions INSERT — only in test. No production writer in python/omp-work! The only INSERT is test seeding. So the approval write path doesn't exist in this repo commit? Wait — the review scope is just these two files, but a finding: migration creates table, store reads it, but nothing writes it (outside tests). Maybe the write path is elsewhere (omp-flood? another component, or a later commit). Grep wider: whole repo for intake_decisions beyond python/omp-work. My grep covered path python/omp-work only. Let me grep repo root. Also maybe an "answer_intake" command in another module or the flood exporter. If no writer exists in this commit, that's a scope observation (feature flag: hold persists until approve, approve path possibly in later work — OMP-407 may be partial). Report as observation with risk: held items cannot be unheld via API yet. Check for a command type like "answer_intake" or "approve_intake" in store/server.

Step 6: ANY(%s) with str lists — need existing precedent. grep "= ANY(%s)" in store.py to see other uses with uuid str lists.

Also concurrency: _intake_classifications runs inside the same transaction as the tree view read — fine. The hold is derived; export (flood) presumably checks intake_hold flag. 

Step 2 remaining + migration runner (step 3): read database.py bootstrap. Check NO FORCE/FORCE usage in earlier migrations — grep.

Also check: does install_workspace_rls grant anything? It just sets RLS policy. The migration grants SELECT to omp_work_readonly but NOT to omp_work_backup — earlier tables (0025-0028) grant SELECT to omp_work_backup too. 0029 omits omp_work_backup! Check: 0028 pattern "GRANT SELECT ... TO omp_work_readonly, omp_work_backup". 0029 only grants readonly. Does omp_work_backup exist and need to read intake_decisions for backups? Check role existence and convention — e.g., 0017 authorization_uses grants. Let me check 0017's grants (line 26 REVOKE ALL, then?). Read 0017 fully. If backup role routinely gets SELECT, omission in 0029 = gap (backup misses decisions or backup fails? pg_dump as backup role without SELECT → table data skipped/error). Actually pg_dump by a non-superuser without SELECT on a table errors or skips. If backup role dumps schema, missing grant → backup silently lacks intake_decisions or errors. That's a real finding if convention holds.

Also TRUNCATE privilege revoke: default roles don't have it anyway; harmless.

CHECK (answer='approve') — only approvals recorded; no reject path. Design: hold persists until approve. Fine, note.

answered_by text — convention: domain_events has actor_id uuid + actor_kind text. intake_decisions.answered_by text vs actor uuid — slight deviation but matches 'owner' seeding in test. Minor.

Also operation_id NOT NULL without FK — idempotent_commands has operation_id too; operations may not be a table. Check whether an operations table exists (operations_evidence? omp_control.operations?). grep "CREATE TABLE.*operations" in migrations. If no operations table, no FK possible — fine.

NO FORCE bracket reason: migration runs as which role? If migrations run as owner role with FORCE RLS on work_items, ADD COLUMN isn't affected by RLS anyway. But maybe install_workspace_rls on intake_decisions references... no. Actually why bracket? Possibly because the migration role is not the table owner and RLS... ALTER TABLE requires ownership regardless. The bracket is harmless but unnecessary — check earlier migrations for precedent: grep "NO FORCE" in migrations dir.

